= Falsifiability — Simple =

A claim is '''falsifiable''' if some possible observation or result could show that it is wrong.

For example:

> All swans are white.

A single real non-white swan would be a [[Counterexample/Simple|counterexample]].

Falsifiable does not mean false.

A claim can be falsifiable and survive many tests.

Karl Popper argued that science should make risky claims that could fail tests instead of explaining every possible result after it happens.

Real science is more complicated than the simple logic.

If an experiment disagrees with a theory, the problem might be:
* the theory;
* the instrument;
* the measurement;
* another assumption;
* the data analysis.

So one strange result does not always instantly falsify a whole theory.

Falsifiability is important in science, but it is not the only thing that makes scientific inquiry scientific.

